1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
 Now let's have a look at the real-life case study about an underground pipeline.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,000
 The case is taken from the American Institute for Chemical Engineers.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
 What is the problem at hand?

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:25,000
 A company has an underground pipeline and they are afraid of potential leakages of flammable and toxic materials in these old pipelines.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:46,000
 So they were thinking about alternatives and alternatives could be to do nothing, hope that nothing happens, to replace the old pipeline or to install valves or not to use pipelines at all and to use trucks to transport the liquids.

6
00:00:47,000 --> 00:01:10,000
 So a first brainstorm gave that list of alternatives but a feasibility check of it already eliminated this one, the truck, because there would be a need for too many trucks going up and down so that's not feasible.

7
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
 Okay, so that's our filter that we talked about.

8
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:23,000
 So we start with the brainstorm session here and for the further analysis we will only pick the ones that are feasible or look feasible at first sight.

9
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:29,000
 Then we have to think about our decision situation, what do we need to take into account?

10
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:37,000
 Here you see, well, we have a risk of pipeline failure, whether or not the failure would result in a slow leak or a rupture.

11
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,000
 What about the amount of chemicals released during such a failure?

12
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:47,000
 The risk of ignition, is there a rupture because we are talking about flammable liquids?

13
00:01:47,000 --> 00:02:00,000
 And then the cost to clean up after a release, toxic material and we have to clean up after, well, there was a spill.

14
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
 Okay, now what are the concerns?

15
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:09,000
 Let's think about, well, the cost of modifying the pipeline, the cost of cleaning up.

16
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:21,000
 We could be concerned about the workers safety, about public safety or our image that public is concerned about, well, having a pipeline underground with these dangerous liquids going into it.

17
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,000
 Now here we decided or rather the company decided to only look at the cost.

18
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:34,000
 So a pure economic decision in the text, it's called net present value, that's what we call present worth.

19
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,000
 We know that if we want to do an investment analysis, we will need some more information.

20
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,000
 We assume that we will work with a horizon of 10 years.

21
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
 We will have to work with expected values.

22
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:55,000
 The salvage value of the old pipeline is zero after 10 years.

23
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:11,000
 And we assume, which is not technically correct, but we assume that the probability of having an ignition and the cost of fire is not a function of the amount of chemicals released, which is not realistic, but that's what we assume here.

24
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:17,000
 OK, here again, we will make use of an influence diagram.

25
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:26,000
 So we have our value model, which here is a net present value of the total cost, present worth.

26
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
 These are the choices that we have to make.

27
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
 Do nothing, replace the pipeline or insert faults.

28
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:43,000
 OK, now what are the things that make up our total costs?

29
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:52,000
 We will have a cleanup cost, can have an influence, the cost of a fire, the cost of pipeline action and the value of the remaining life of the pipeline.

30
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:05,000
 These value measures are determined by the type of failure in the quantity released, cleanup cost, ignition, years until failure.

31
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:13,000
 And we also have an influence, of course, from this on these things, on the quantity release.

32
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,000
 Well, the type of failure, we have nothing to do, but these things are influence.

33
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
 So we have an influence.

34
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
 Now we're ready to do some computations.

35
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:41,000
 We have our value function, which is here a cost function with components, pipeline action, cleanup, fire, value of remaining life, our salvage value and the cost of cleanup is determined by

36
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:47,000
 the cost per gallon times the amount of gallons that is released.

37
00:04:47,000 --> 00:05:04,000
 Interesting in the data collection is that part of our data we can consider to be certain, deterministic, another type of data or another number of data points of parameters or not deterministic.

38
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
 So we have some probability.

39
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:13,000
 OK, now we will first take the nominal scenario.

40
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:21,000
 So we are calculating the expected value and we see that here we see the outcome.

41
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:27,000
 Now, if you carry out the sensitivity analysis, we will visualize it in a tornado diagram.

42
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:31,000
 Tornado diagram is an interesting visualization.

43
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,000
 We will see another example later on.

44
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,000
 What is the tornado diagram?

45
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:56,000
 A tornado diagram is a diagram that is showing us the nominal situation and then is taking on the variables one by one and we compute their impact from the lowest value, the highest value and see how large the impact is.

46
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:01,000
 The one with the largest impact comes on top of this graph.

47
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:15,000
 The other one, the one with the lowest impact goes for the magnitude of impact in terms of magnitude of total cost comes at the bottom.

48
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
 Not only that is interesting, it's also interesting.

49
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:24,000
 That's what's here is whether or not that would change our decision.

50
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:43,000
 And here you see that, for instance, you see clearly that the decision will change if that value of the parameter years until failure is on the lowest value or nominal or higher value.

51
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:56,000
 Now probabilistic phase, we will build our decision tree taking into account probabilities and then we see that we have the lowest value here for insert faults.

52
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:01,000
 We can do some more risk profiles, but let's skip that for the moment.

53
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:08,000
 We can compute the value of information, the value of control.

54
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:13,000
 There's some information in the text about it, but it's not complete to do the computations.

55
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,000
 But let's assume that they did not make any mistakes.

56
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,000
 So what are the conclusions that we can draw from this rough study?

57
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:31,000
 If immediate action is required and there's no possibility for further study, then we would install the valves.

58
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:41,000
 If no intermediate action is necessary, we could try to obtain more information on the failure probability of the current pipeline, which would tell us something about the years to failure.

59
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:46,000
 And we know that the maximum cost for that is 111,000.

60
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:53,000
 We could also consider a monitoring system to limit the slow leak.

61
00:07:53,000 --> 00:08:01,000
 And the maximum cost for that, as we just computed, is 1.2 million.

62
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:06,000
 Now, what do we do in real life?

63
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:13,000
 Well, if no immediate action is required, so this is what we saw from our study, this is what the company in question did.

64
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:25,000
 So as no immediate action was required, they ordered an ultrasonic study of the current pipeline.

65
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:29,000
 It only costed 20,000, so that's fine.

66
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:33,000
 That's less than what we had here.

67
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:36,000
 And they fed that into the model.

68
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:40,000
 The monitoring system they found quite interesting.

69
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,000
 And they added to the list of feasibility.

70
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:47,000
 So now they have four alternatives to decide instead of three.

71
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:51,000
 They tried to refine the probabilities on the type of failure.

72
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:54,000
 Also, that information was fed into the model.

73
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:02,000
 And they were thinking of an early warning system so that they could reduce some of the uncertainty.

74
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
 Also, that was fed to the model.

75
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:10,000
 And then everything could be updated and the best alternative could be selected.

76
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:16,000
 Best, again, here we are talking about euros or in this case dollars.

77
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:22,000
 So, for instance, worker safety or image were not considered.

78
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:28,000
 I think it's a nice case study showing that decision trees are indeed used.

79
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:34,000
 Also showing again the use of something like an influence diagram and a tornado diagram.

80
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:40,000
 Now, about this tornado diagram, why is it so nice?

81
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:44,000
 I think it's nice because it's visualizing the sensitivity analysis.

82
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
 And this is taken from a PhD study.

83
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:53,000
 So these are the parameters that are considered for sensitivity testing.

84
00:09:53,000 --> 00:10:04,000
 They are changed one by one and then they are displayed in impact of change in they bring to the nominal scenario.

85
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:11,000
 Maybe something here that I should have mentioned also is that we assume that these are independent.

86
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:16,000
 So we change them one by one and we assume that these parameters are independent.

87
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:22,000
 This is a clear requirement.

88
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:25,000
 This is another example.

89
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:33,000
 Cost effectiveness of a national telemedicine diabetic retinopathy screening program in Singapore.

90
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:38,000
 We will talk about cost effectiveness analysis when we have the chapter on cost benefit.

91
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:45,000
 Here also we have a tornado diagram and here this is the incremental effectiveness ratio.

92
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:50,000
 So you see it's actually something that is quite often used.

93
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:54,000
 Okay, then we are at the end of the chapter.

94
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
 These are the exercises.

95
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:05,000
 As usual, we will solve them by hand later on.

96
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,000
 There's a sensitivity analysis to the economic environment study.

97
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
 And this is what a longer exercise.

98
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:16,000
 Maybe you can have a look at it and for once you can neglect the time value of money.

99
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
 But that's very exceptional.

100
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:26,000
 But the purpose of this exercise is to show again how the decision tree system works.

101
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:31,000
 Okay, so that is the end of this chapter.

